name: processes
boss: Daemon
flavor: I started before you logged in. I'll be here after.
brief: Every running program is a process with a number. `ps` lists them, `kill`
brief: sends them a signal, `top` watches them live.
brief:
brief: `kill` does not mean kill. It sends a signal, and the default is `TERM`,
brief: which asks politely and can be caught or ignored. `KILL` (`9`) cannot be
brief: caught, which is why it is a last resort and not a first one -- a
brief: program killed with `9` never gets to clean up after itself.
brief:
brief: Finding the one you want: `pgrep` and `pidof` match by name, `pstree` shows
brief: who started whom, `pwdx` says where a process is standing, `pmap` what it
brief: has mapped, `lsof` and `fuser` name the process holding a file or a mount
brief: open -- which is the answer to most "device is busy" refusals. `pkill`
brief: and `killall` end one by name instead of by number.
brief:
brief: `nproc` counts the cpus, `free` reports memory. `unshare` runs a command in
brief: its own namespaces and `nsenter` joins one already running -- that pair,
brief: and nothing more, is what a container is underneath.
---
q: %list% the processes running right now
a: ps
out: ^PID +USER +TIME +COMMAND$
ref: ps
teach: ps
hint: ps alone shows just this terminal's processes, not the whole system
---
q: %list% processes trimmed to just pid and name, with `ps`
a: ps -o pid,comm
out: ^PID +COMMAND$
ref: ps
teach: ps -o
hint: -o picks exactly which columns to show
